# Line-ending policy. Without this the repo held a mixture: 40 files CRLF, 7
# LF, and two - tools/scrims_push.py and profiles/vanilla.json - mixed inside a
# single file, which is what produced git's recurring "LF will be replaced by
# CRLF" warnings.
#
# text=auto stores text as LF in the repo and converts on checkout according to
# the local core.autocrlf, so Windows working copies still get CRLF.
* text=auto

# Windows-only artefacts, pinned to CRLF wherever they are checked out rather
# than left to the local setting.

# cmd.exe is the one consumer that can genuinely misbehave on LF, around labels
# and goto.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Copied verbatim into the game's config directory by `dimod apply`, so it is
# read by Unreal as a Windows INI rather than by anything in this repo.
*.ini text eol=crlf
baseline/TripwireServer.ini.original text eol=crlf

# The opposite case: sha256sum -c cannot read a CRLF manifest - the carriage
# return lands on the filename and the open fails - which is why
# tools/verify_baseline.py exists. Pinning these to LF makes the standard tool
# work too. See docs/09-native-stage0.md.
*.sha256 text eol=lf
